-Two "\n" characters after each other will create a blank line

Examnple:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
  cout << "Hello World!" << "\n\n";
  cout << "I am learning about C++";
  return 0;
}

// output will be 
    Hello world
    I am learning about C++

The explanation:
-\n is a "control code" that tells the computer: "Stop printing on this line and move the cursor to the beginning of the next line."
-in code, you can't always hit the physical "Enter" key inside a string of text. So, you use \n as a shortcut to say "Enter" inside your text.
